welle

This software solves the wave equation in three dimensions using the
retarded single layer potential and space-time Galerkin discretization
with piecewise constant elements in space and time. The mesh of the
scatterer must be provided. Included are a few refinements of the unit sphere.
The code can be easily adapted to work with other scatterers as well.
The appropriate quadrature rules for the numerical integration are also
provided. 



The code depends on the lapack and blas package which must be
installed. A makefile is provided which should compile the code with
no edits in a gnu/unix environment. Modifications for other systems
should be straight forward. The compile command is

make welle

The behaviour of the code is controlled by command line switches. The general usage is

./welle [OPTION]... [FILE]

where [FILE] is the file that describes the triangulation of the scatterer.

The shell scripts listed below give examples of how to use the code.



Contents
========
quadErrors.sh this shell script prints integrals used to setup the coefficients of the 
              discretized linear system. It is used to test the convergence of the
              quadrature scheme.
solutions.sh  this shell script solves a scattering problem from the unit sphere with
              a known analytic solutions. It can be used to test the convergence of 
              the fully discrete Galerkin method with respect to refininig the discretization
              meshes. The code also prints out more information about the number of non-zero
              matrix entries, CPU times and other resouce metrics if the additional command line
              switch -v=1 is added. Warning: the finer meshes require a large amount of RAM.
quadSoln.sh   this shell script solves the same scattering problem from the unit sphere to test 
              the convergence of the numerical solution with respect to increasing the quadrature
              order.              
welle.c       main file
mesh.c        routines for reading the mesh
polytope.c    routines for polytope calculations
quadFFR.c     quadrature routines for singular integrals
quadRule.c    routines for reading quadrature rules
kernelWelle.c implements the kernel of the time-integrated wave equation
solver.c      linear algebra routines

Meshes

This directory contains several triangulations of the unit sphere. The file name for the vertex coordinates
ends with "V" and the file name for the faces ends with F. The numbers 0 to 3 in the file name refer to the
refinement level. To display these triangulations in Matlab use the following commands:


load sphere1V
load sphere1F
sphere1F = sphere1F + 1;
patch('Vertices', sphere1V, 'Faces', sphere1F, 'FaceColor', 'white', 'EdgeColor', 'black' );



QuadRules

This directory contains all quadrature rules that are used by the code. There are two types of rules

* Gauss-Jacobi Rules:

    \int^0_1 f(x) x^alpha (1-x)^beta dx = \sum_k f(x_k) w_k  +  error

  The rules are stored in files named  quad_GJ_a<alpha>_b<beta>_deg<degree>.txt 
  where deg is the degree of precision. Note that the directory contains only odd-degree rules as there
  are no even degree rules.
  The rules were generated by a package by Pierre Asselin which apparently has disappeared from the intenet.
  However, there are many other ways to obtain these rules.


* Gauss-like quadrature rules for the two and three dimensional unit simplex
  
  The rules are stored in files named quad_simplex_deg<degree>_dim<d>.txt
  Here the degree is the largest degree of two or three variate polynomials that are integrated exactly.
  The rules were generated by the package gen-quad https://github.com/arkslobodkins/gen-quad, however,
  the nodes in the files are expressed in barycentric coordinates, that is, the nodes satisfy

            x_k >= 0  and \sum_{k=0}^d x_k = 1


Data

This directory contains the outputs of the three shell scripts listed above.
